items={
'72bcc3':(6.5,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Colouring','Extremal arguments','Modular arithmetic'],'A maximal reachable set can be enlarged by repeatedly shifting every outside colour by −m; checking that the proper colouring survives is the key step, while a complete graph supplies necessity.',r'A finite connected graph admits a proper colouring by residues modulo $2024$, and a starting vertex is fixed. For which $m\in\{1,\ldots,2024\}$ can one always choose such a colouring so that every vertex is reachable from the start along edges whose colour increase is $m$ modulo $2024$?',r'The answer is $\gcd(m,2024)=1$. Necessity follows from the complete graph on $2024$ vertices: all colours must occur, whereas directed walks stay in one coset of the subgroup generated by $m$.'+'\n\n'+r'For sufficiency, choose a proper colouring maximizing the set $W$ reachable from the start. Suppose $W$ is proper. Subtract $m$ from all colours outside $W$. Colours within either part remain distinct across edges. If a crossing edge became monochromatic, its former outside colour exceeded its inside colour by $m$, so its outside endpoint would already have been reachable. Thus the shifted colouring is proper. Reachability within $W$ persists, and maximality says no outside vertex becomes reachable. Repeat this shift. Connectivity supplies a crossing edge; because $m$ is coprime to $2024$, its outside colour eventually exceeds its inside colour by $m$. This contradicts maximality.',['Graph theory','Colouring','Extremal arguments']),
'a93650':(5,'Algebra',['Functional equations','Injectivity','Surjectivity'],'Substituting a zero of f makes f composed with itself a scalar multiple of the identity; a nonzero scalar gives injectivity, while the zero case uses forced surjectivity to eliminate every nonzero value.',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ for which $$f(f(x)+xf(y))=xf(y+1)\qquad(x,y\in\mathbb R).$$',r'Put $a=f(0)$. Taking $x=0$ gives $f(a)=0$, and taking $y=a$ gives $f(f(x))=cx$, where $c=f(a+1)$.'+'\n\n'+r'If $c\ne0$, then $f$ is injective. Taking $x=1$ in the original identity and using injectivity gives $f(1)+f(y)=y+1$. At $y=1$ this yields $f(1)=1$, hence $f(y)=y$.'+'\n\n'+r'If $c=0$, then $f$ vanishes on its image. If $f(y_0+1)\ne0$ for some $y_0$, the original identity with $y=y_0$ has right side ranging over all real numbers as $x$ varies. Hence $f$ is surjective and therefore vanishes everywhere, a contradiction. Thus $f\equiv0$. Both $f(x)=x$ and $f(x)=0$ satisfy the identity.',['Functional equations']),
'6ce436':(7,'Number Theory',['Pigeonhole principle','Cubes','Integer bounds','Factorisation'],'After choosing adjacent elements with ratio at most two, four hypothetical cubes satisfy a product identity; an explicit cube-gap bound forces equal products, then equal sums contradict strict interlacing.',r'Let $n\ge4$, let $A\subseteq\{1,\ldots,2^n\}$ have $n+1$ elements, and let $B\subseteq\{2^{n-1}+1,\ldots,2^n\}$ have two elements. Can every number $ab-1$, with $a\in A$ and $b\in B$, be a perfect cube?',r"""No. In increasing order, two consecutive elements $a<a'$ of $A$ satisfy $a'\le2a$; otherwise the largest exceeds $2^n$. Write $B=\{b<b'\}$, so $b'<2b$. Suppose $$ab-1=q^3,\quad a'b'-1=r^3,\quad ab'-1=s^3,\quad a'b-1=t^3.$$ Then $q<s,t<r<2q$, and $$ (qr)^3+q^3+r^3=(st)^3+s^3+t^3.$$ If $qr\ne st$, the cube difference has magnitude greater than $3q^2(q+2)^2$, since both products are at least $q(q+2)$. The remaining sum difference has magnitude less than $2(r^3-q^3)<14q^3$. This is impossible because $3(q+2)^2>14q$. Therefore $qr=st$ and $q^3+r^3=s^3+t^3$. Equal sums and products force $\{q^3,r^3\}=\{s^3,t^3\}$, contradicting the strict inequalities.""",['Squares & powers','Divisibility & GCD','Pigeonhole principle']),
'bb6a28':(6,'Geometry',['Coordinates','Reflection','Circumcentre','Locus'],'The altitude feet lie on the fixed circle with diameter BC; reflecting the circumcenter in the side lines gives a common intersection at the inverse of that circumcenter in this circle.',r'Fix segment $BC$ and vary $A$ so that $ABC$ is acute. Let $P\in AC$ and $Q\in AB$ be its altitude feet from $B$ and $C$, and let $O$ be its circumcenter. Reflect $O$ in $AB$ and $AC$ to obtain $K$ and $L$. The line through $P$ perpendicular to $LP$ meets the line through $Q$ perpendicular to $KQ$ at $T$. Prove that $T$ lies on a fixed line.',r'Scale and rotate so $B=(-1,0)$, $C=(1,0)$ and $O=(0,w)$. Acuteness gives $w\ne0$. We prove $T=(0,1/w)$, which lies on the fixed perpendicular bisector of $BC$.'+'\n\n'+r'Write $AC$ as $y=m(x-1)$. Projection of $B$ onto this line gives $$P=(p_x,p_y)=\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+1},\frac{-2m}{m^2+1}\right),\qquad p_x^2+p_y^2=1.$$ Reflecting $O$ in that line and subtracting $P$ gives $$L-P=(1-wp_y,wp_x).$$ Hence $P\cdot(L-P)=p_x=(0,1/w)\cdot(L-P)$. Thus $(0,1/w)$ lies on the required perpendicular through $P$. The same calculation with $B$ and $C$ interchanged proves it lies on the required perpendicular through $Q$, completing the proof.',['Coordinates & vectors','Transformations','Constructions & loci'])}
