# Resolve the previously proofless Greek selection geometry problem independently.
slug='grc_2024_290a85';r[slug]={'difficulty':5.5,'topic':'Geometry','tags':['Coordinates','Circumcentres','Midpoints','Dot products'],'reason':'The shared circumcenter and two right angles give orthogonal projection formulas for B and E; comparing their midpoint dot products with those of the second circumcenter proves collinearity.','competition':'Greek IMO Selection Test','section':'B. Seniors','number':'2','sourceUrl':'https://mathnet.mit.edu/explorer.html?p='+slug,'solutionUrl':'https://mathnet.mit.edu/explorer.html?p='+slug,'statement':r'In a convex pentagon $ABCDE$, $\angle ABC=\angle AED=90^\circ$. The midpoint of $CD$ is the circumcenter of $ABE$. If $O$ is the circumcenter of $ACD$ and $N$ is the midpoint of $BE$, prove that $A,O,N$ are collinear.','solution':r'Take $A$ as the vector origin, with lower-case letters denoting position vectors. Put $U=c\cdot c$, $V=d\cdot d$ and $t=c\cdot d$. The circumcenter condition gives $b\cdot b=(c+d)\cdot b$, while the right angle at $B$ gives $b\cdot b=c\cdot b$. Hence $d\cdot b=0$. Since $b\ne0$, these conditions identify the projection $$b=c-\frac tVd.$$ Similarly $$e=d-\frac tUc.$$ Thus, writing $\Delta=UV-t^2>0$, $$n\cdot c=\frac{\Delta}{2V},\qquad n\cdot d=\frac{\Delta}{2U}.$$ On the other hand, $o\cdot c=U/2$ and $o\cdot d=V/2$. Since $c,d$ are independent, these dot products imply $$n=\frac{\Delta}{UV}o.$$ Therefore $A,N,O$ are collinear.','solutionKind':'original-exposition','subtopics':['Coordinates & vectors','Triangle centres','Concurrency & collinearity'],'license':'Original contest rights retained; concise paraphrase and original exposition.','notes':'Independent vector proof resolves the previously missing solution; source is the Hellenic Mathematical Society 2023–24 booklet, Selection Examination, Seniors problem 2.'}
(p/'reviews.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
